{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'rg -n \"\" docs/design/datom.world.md | head -180'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'rg -n \"\" docs/design/datom.world.md | head -180'","aggregated_output":"1:---\n2:description: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n3:---\n4:\n5:# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n6:\n7:## Core Philosophy\n8:\n9:Everything is data.\n10:Interpretation > abstraction.\n11:Explicit causality > implicit assumptions.\n12:Restrictions are a feature.\n13:\n14:### Foundational Axioms\n15:\n16:1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n17:2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n18:   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n19:   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n20:   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n21:3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n22:4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n23:\n24:## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n25:\n26:- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n27:- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n28:- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n29:- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n30:- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n31:- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n32:\n33:## Tuples and Datoms\n34:\n35:Tuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\n36:A datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\n37:Source scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n38:\n39:See [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n40:\n41:## Streams\n42:\n43:All IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\n44:No direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\n45:Side effects must appear as stream emissions.\n46:Streams are values that can be sent through streams.\n47:Streams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n48:\n49:A stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\n50:of interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\n51:semantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\n52:stream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\n53:the very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\n54:answers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\n55:exists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\n56:the other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\n57:and the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\n58:becomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\n59:the stream's own.\n60:\n61:Functions can take anything and return anything.\n62:Agents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n63:\n64:See [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\n65:See [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n66:\n67:### Host Boundaries\n68:\n69:A host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\n70:sanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\n71:exhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\n72:\n73:**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\n74:happen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\n75:appends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\n76:identity in the data; neither side holds a reference to the other. A host with\n77:no implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a\n78:correct outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n79:\n80:**Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\n81:a timer fires) the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\n82:nothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\n83:Constructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\n84:to data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n85:\n86:**Adapters.** An adapter is a map of those transform functions. That is all an\n87:adapter is. It is a value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files,\n88:timers and windows all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is\n89:what they call. Each entry takes the host's arguments and returns one value;\n90:the append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry cannot return a value\n91:to the host and cannot invoke application code. The adapter is handed only the\n92:deposit operation and its own host resource. If its deposit channel fails, it\n93:may close that resource to make the boundary observably gone; this is teardown,\n94:not an upward callback.\n95:\n96:Host types are classified into plain data in the transform: it is the last\n97:place holding a host error object, and no host type crosses onto the stream. A\n98:transport may use a qualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and\n99:additive evolution; that envelope remains plain data and creates no separate\n100:event mechanism. A composition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary\n101:for facts it owns.\n102:\n103:Which stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\n104:adapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\n105:transport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read. This keeps\n106:the layering one-way: a boundary depends on nothing above it, because whoever\n107:constructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n108:\n109:A transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\n110:none of them semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol validation\n111:happen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport machinery, not an\n112:event. Once the transport has produced a valid host event, data is syntax\n113:(axiom 2): an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to another that\n114:does not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys perspectives\n115:not yet taken. Retention is the medium's operational policy; meaning is\n116:interpretation. Neither is the depositor's decision.\n117:\n118:Two things are not adapters. One that exposes a function for portable code to\n119:call isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the effect never\n120:appears as an emission. One that takes a function to invoke has relocated the\n121:callback, not removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data; if\n122:that callback has a meaningful return value, it is a synchronous call in both\n123:directions wearing the shape of an event.\n124:\n125:## Architectural Commitments\n126:\n127:- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n128:- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n129:- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n130:- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n131:\n132:## Design Principles\n133:\n134:Code quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\n135:Stigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n136:\n137:**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\n138:composition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\n139:without collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n140:\n141:- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n142:  ordinary streams.\n143:- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n144:  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n145:- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n146:- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n147:  auto-index observer.\n148:- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n149:  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n150:\n151:**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\n152:canonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\n153:shape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n154:`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\n155:query or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\n156:consumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\n157:retired `:global` tag in\n158:[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\n159:case study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n160:\n161:See [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\n162:See [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n163:\n164:## Code Style\n165:\n166:- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n167:- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n168:- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n169:- Avoid cleverness.\n170:- Name things after what they do to streams.\n171:\n172:## Development Workflow\n173:\n174:- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n175:- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n176:- Do not optimize prematurely.\n177:- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n178:- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n179:- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n180:  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
