{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '140,520p' src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc | nl -ba -v 140\"","aggregated_output":"   140\t;; =============================================================================\n   141\t;; Explicit client state\n   142\t;; =============================================================================\n   143\t\n   144\t(defn- random-safe-id\n   145\t  []\n   146\t  #?(:clj (long (* max-safe-id (rand)))\n   147\t     :cljs (Math/floor (* max-safe-id (rand)))\n   148\t     :cljd (.floor ^double (* max-safe-id (rand)))))\n   149\t\n   150\t\n   151\t(defn client-state\n   152\t  \"Create caller-owned RPC client state.\n   153\t\n   154\t   `writer` is the request path. `reader` and `response-cursor` are the\n   155\t   independent response path.  No cursor is fabricated here; a caller\n   156\t   over a dao.stream.remote reflection passes `stream/anchor-newest`\n   157\t   (the request/response service's own mint, docs/design/\n   158\t   dao.stream.remote.md section 5) and `poll!` resolves it, retrying\n   159\t   while the reflection answers a retryable mint -- the same pattern\n   160\t   `dao.jing.content.step/mint-cursor` uses for the content client.\"\n   161\t  ([writer reader response-cursor]\n   162\t   (client-state writer reader response-cursor {}))\n   163\t  ([writer reader response-cursor _opts]\n   164\t   {:writer writer\n   165\t    :reader reader\n   166\t    :cursor response-cursor\n   167\t    :outstanding {}\n   168\t    :unsent nil\n   169\t    :completed []\n   170\t    :diagnostics []\n   171\t    :terminal nil}))\n   172\t\n   173\t\n   174\t(def init-client client-state)\n   175\t\n   176\t\n   177\t(defn- rpc-result\n   178\t  [outcome state & {:as extra}]\n   179\t  (merge {:dao.stream.rpc/outcome outcome\n   180\t          :dao.stream.rpc/state state}\n   181\t         extra))\n   182\t\n   183\t\n   184\t(defn- valid-operation-result\n   185\t  [op result]\n   186\t  (if (stream/valid-outcome? op result)\n   187\t    result\n   188\t    {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n   189\t     :dao.stream/error :dao.stream/invalid-operation-result}))\n   190\t\n   191\t\n   192\t(defn- completion\n   193\t  [request & {:keys [response reason]}]\n   194\t  (cond-> {:dao.stream.rpc/id (:id request)\n   195\t           :dao.stream.rpc/op (:op request)\n   196\t           :dao.stream.rpc/args (:args request)}\n   197\t    response (assoc :dao.stream.rpc/response response)\n   198\t    reason (assoc :dao.stream.rpc/reason reason)))\n   199\t\n   200\t\n   201\t(defn- append-completion\n   202\t  [state request & {:as fields}]\n   203\t  (update state :completed conj (apply completion request (mapcat identity fields))))\n   204\t\n   205\t\n   206\t(defn- append-diagnostic\n   207\t  [state code value]\n   208\t  (update state :diagnostics conj {code-key code\n   209\t                                   :dao.stream.rpc/value value}))\n   210\t\n   211\t\n   212\t(defn- ids-in-use?\n   213\t  [state id]\n   214\t  (or (= id (get-in state [:unsent :id]))\n   215\t      (contains? (:outstanding state) id)\n   216\t      (boolean (some #(= id (:dao.stream.rpc/id %)) (:completed state)))))\n   217\t\n   218\t\n   219\t;; Defined below with the other completion bookkeeping; allocation failure is\n   220\t;; a terminal transition and owes the same conservative loss as every other.\n   221\t(declare lose-outstanding)\n   222\t\n   223\t\n   224\t(defn- allocation-failure\n   225\t  [state code]\n   226\t  (let [state (-> state\n   227\t                  (lose-outstanding :dao.stream.rpc/allocator-error true)\n   228\t                  (append-diagnostic code nil))]\n   229\t    (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/allocator-error state\n   230\t                :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic (last (:diagnostics state)))))\n   231\t\n   232\t\n   233\t(defn- allocate-request\n   234\t  \"Self-mint a random safe id in [0, max-safe-id], re-rolling on a collision\n   235\t   with an id already in use by this state, up to `max-id-retries` times.\n   236\t   This is the request/answer service's own id space (docs/design/\n   237\t   dao.stream.remote.md section 5): callers sharing one answers stream must\n   238\t   not collide on a caller-chosen id, so the id is not a sequential counter.\"\n   239\t  [state op args]\n   240\t  (loop [attempt 0]\n   241\t    (if (>= attempt max-id-retries)\n   242\t      (allocation-failure state :dao.stream.rpc/id-collision)\n   243\t      (let [id (random-safe-id)]\n   244\t        (if (ids-in-use? state id)\n   245\t          (recur (inc attempt))\n   246\t          (let [request {:id id\n   247\t                         :op op\n   248\t                         :args args\n   249\t                         :encoded (request-value id op args)}]\n   250\t            (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/allocated\n   251\t                        (assoc state :unsent request))))))))\n   252\t\n   253\t\n   254\t(defn- accept-unsent\n   255\t  [state request]\n   256\t  (-> state\n   257\t      (assoc :unsent nil)\n   258\t      (assoc-in [:outstanding (:id request)]\n   259\t                {:op (:op request) :args (:args request)})))\n   260\t\n   261\t\n   262\t(defn- attempt-unsent\n   263\t  [state]\n   264\t  (let [request (:unsent state)\n   265\t        append-result (valid-operation-result\n   266\t                        :append!\n   267\t                        (stream/append! (:writer state) (:encoded request)))\n   268\t        outcome (:dao.stream/outcome append-result)]\n   269\t    (case outcome\n   270\t      :dao.stream/ok\n   271\t      (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/requested\n   272\t                  (accept-unsent state request)\n   273\t                  :dao.stream.rpc/id (:id request)\n   274\t                  :dao.stream.rpc/append append-result)\n   275\t\n   276\t      :dao.stream/full\n   277\t      (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/pending-request state\n   278\t                  :dao.stream.rpc/id (:id request)\n   279\t                  :dao.stream.rpc/append append-result)\n   280\t\n   281\t      (:dao.stream/closed :dao.stream/invalid-value :dao.stream/transport-error)\n   282\t      (let [state (-> state\n   283\t                      (assoc :unsent nil)\n   284\t                      (append-completion request :reason outcome))]\n   285\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/request-undeliverable state\n   286\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/id (:id request)\n   287\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/reason outcome\n   288\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/append append-result))\n   289\t\n   290\t      ;; A malformed writer answer becomes transport-error in\n   291\t      ;; valid-operation-result, so this branch is defensive only.\n   292\t      (let [state (-> state\n   293\t                      (assoc :unsent nil)\n   294\t                      (append-completion request :reason :dao.stream/transport-error))]\n   295\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/request-undeliverable state\n   296\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/id (:id request)\n   297\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/reason :dao.stream/transport-error\n   298\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/append append-result)))))\n   299\t\n   300\t\n   301\t(defn request!\n   302\t  \"Attempt one request append, returning an explicit next state.\n   303\t\n   304\t   If a previous append was full, this retries the exact already-allocated\n   305\t   envelope and ignores `op`/`args`; it never allocates a replacement id.  The\n   306\t   function makes one append attempt at most and never spins on `:full`.\"\n   307\t  [state op args]\n   308\t  (cond\n   309\t    (:terminal state)\n   310\t    (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/terminal state)\n   311\t\n   312\t    (:unsent state)\n   313\t    (attempt-unsent state)\n   314\t\n   315\t    (not (and (keyword? op) (vector? args)))\n   316\t    (let [state (append-diagnostic state :dao.stream.rpc/invalid-request\n   317\t                                   {:op op :args args})]\n   318\t      (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/invalid-request state\n   319\t                  :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic (last (:diagnostics state))))\n   320\t\n   321\t    :else\n   322\t    (let [allocated (allocate-request state op args)]\n   323\t      (if (= :dao.stream.rpc/allocated (:dao.stream.rpc/outcome allocated))\n   324\t        (attempt-unsent (:dao.stream.rpc/state allocated))\n   325\t        allocated))))\n   326\t\n   327\t\n   328\t(defn unsent?\n   329\t  \"True while an allocated request is still owed one accepted append.  A caller\n   330\t   that allocates ids from this state must not submit new work while it holds:\n   331\t   `request!` retries the unsent envelope and ignores the operation it is given.\"\n   332\t  [state]\n   333\t  (some? (:unsent state)))\n   334\t\n   335\t\n   336\t(defn abandon-unsent\n   337\t  \"Give up on an allocated-but-unsent request, appending its completion with\n   338\t   `reason`.\n   339\t\n   340\t   A composition that changes what the writer *is* — an operator disconnect, a\n   341\t   rebind onto a fresh attachment — owns the decision that the retained\n   342\t   envelope no longer belongs to the new binding.  Abandoning it here reports\n   343\t   the loss on the ordinary completion path, so it is neither silently dropped\n   344\t   nor resent in place of the caller's next request.\"\n   345\t  ([state] (abandon-unsent state :dao.stream.rpc/abandoned))\n   346\t  ([state reason]\n   347\t   (if-let [request (:unsent state)]\n   348\t     (-> state\n   349\t         (assoc :unsent nil)\n   350\t         (append-completion request :reason reason))\n   351\t     state)))\n   352\t\n   353\t\n   354\t;; =============================================================================\n   355\t;; Response decoding and correlation\n   356\t;; =============================================================================\n   357\t\n   358\t(defn- normalize-event\n   359\t  [value]\n   360\t  (cond\n   361\t    (nil? value) (ignore-event)\n   362\t    (answer? value) (response-event value)\n   363\t    (and (map? value) (keyword? (get value event-key))) value\n   364\t    :else (diagnostic-event :dao.stream.rpc/malformed-response value)))\n   365\t\n   366\t\n   367\t(defn- decode-value\n   368\t  [_state value]\n   369\t  (try\n   370\t    (normalize-event value)\n   371\t    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _\n   372\t      (diagnostic-event :dao.stream.rpc/decode-error value))))\n   373\t\n   374\t\n   375\t(defn- lose-outstanding\n   376\t  [state reason terminal?]\n   377\t  (let [state (reduce (fn [s [id request]]\n   378\t                        (append-completion s (assoc request :id id) :reason reason))\n   379\t                      state\n   380\t                      (:outstanding state))]\n   381\t    (cond-> (assoc state :outstanding {})\n   382\t      terminal? (assoc :terminal reason))))\n   383\t\n   384\t\n   385\t(defn handle-event\n   386\t  \"Apply one decoded RPC event to explicit client state.\n   387\t\n   388\t   `response-event`, `diagnostic-event`, and `ignore-event` are the events a\n   389\t   composition constructs; a bare answer value is also accepted.  This\n   390\t   function has no cursor operation and is useful for testing adapters\n   391\t   independently from a response medium.\"\n   392\t  [state event]\n   393\t  (let [event (normalize-event event)\n   394\t        kind (get event event-key)]\n   395\t    (cond\n   396\t      ;; A terminal binding cannot complete an old request later.  Keep the\n   397\t      ;; event as diagnostic data rather than silently treating it as a reply.\n   398\t      (:terminal state)\n   399\t      (let [state (append-diagnostic state :dao.stream.rpc/event-after-terminal event)]\n   400\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic state\n   401\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic (last (:diagnostics state))))\n   402\t\n   403\t      (= kind :dao.stream.rpc/ignore)\n   404\t      (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/ignored state)\n   405\t\n   406\t      (= kind :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic)\n   407\t      (let [diagnostic (get event diagnostic-key)\n   408\t            state (append-diagnostic state (get diagnostic code-key) diagnostic)]\n   409\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic state\n   410\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic (last (:diagnostics state))))\n   411\t\n   412\t      (= kind :dao.stream.rpc/response)\n   413\t      (let [response (get event response-key)]\n   414\t        (if-not (answer? response)\n   415\t          (handle-event state (diagnostic-event :dao.stream.rpc/malformed-response response))\n   416\t          (let [id (answer-id response)]\n   417\t            (if-let [request (get (:outstanding state) id)]\n   418\t              (let [state (-> state\n   419\t                              (update :outstanding dissoc id)\n   420\t                              (append-completion (assoc request :id id)\n   421\t                                                 :response response))]\n   422\t                (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/responded state\n   423\t                            :dao.stream.rpc/id id\n   424\t                            :dao.stream.rpc/response response))\n   425\t              (handle-event state\n   426\t                            (diagnostic-event :dao.stream.rpc/unsolicited-response response))))))\n   427\t\n   428\t      :else\n   429\t      (handle-event state (diagnostic-event :dao.stream.rpc/unhandled-event event)))))\n   430\t\n   431\t\n   432\t(defn- transport-error-reason\n   433\t  \"Translate a reflection's `:dao.stream/transport-error` into the client\n   434\t   terminal vocabulary, inspecting `:dao.stream.remote/reason` when the\n   435\t   reflection supplied one (dao.stream.remote.cljc reasons a reflection's\n   436\t   read failure this way).  A reason this translation does not recognize\n   437\t   still becomes the generic transport-error terminal, never left raw.\"\n   438\t  [read-result]\n   439\t  (case (:dao.stream.remote/reason read-result)\n   440\t    :dao.stream.remote/not-found :dao.stream.apply/not-found\n   441\t    :dao.stream.remote/channel-gone :dao.stream.apply/detached\n   442\t    :dao.stream.apply/transport-error))\n   443\t\n   444\t\n   445\t(defn- terminal-lost\n   446\t  \"Translate a non-ok, non-blocked, non-gap read or mint result into the\n   447\t   client's terminal vocabulary and complete every outstanding request\n   448\t   with it.  Shared by `poll-read`'s `next` failures and `mint-cursor`'s\n   449\t   terminal mint failures, so a reasoned not-found or channel-gone\n   450\t   reaches the terminal the same way regardless of which operation\n   451\t   the reflection answered it to.\"\n   452\t  [state result]\n   453\t  (let [reason (case (:dao.stream/outcome result)\n   454\t                 :dao.stream/end :dao.stream.apply/ended\n   455\t                 :dao.stream/transport-error (transport-error-reason result)\n   456\t                 :dao.stream.apply/transport-error)\n   457\t        state (lose-outstanding state reason true)]\n   458\t    (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/lost state\n   459\t                :dao.stream.rpc/reason reason\n   460\t                :dao.stream.rpc/read result)))\n   461\t\n   462\t\n   463\t(defn- mint-cursor\n   464\t  \"Resolve an anchor-keyword cursor through the reader.  A retryable\n   465\t   answer -- `:dao.stream/transport-error` carrying `:dao.stream/retry?\n   466\t   true` -- leaves the anchor in place for a later poll (dao.jing.content.\n   467\t   step/mint-cursor is the precedent this mirrors).  Any other non-ok\n   468\t   outcome is a reasoned terminal failure: a reflection can answer\n   469\t   `not-found` or `channel-gone` while minting a cursor\n   470\t   (dao.stream.remote.cljc's `refl-cursor`), and that must not be\n   471\t   mistaken for still-pending.  Returns `[status cursor result]`, status\n   472\t   one of `:ok`, `:retry`, `:terminal`; `result` is retained for\n   473\t   diagnostics either way.\"\n   474\t  [reader anchor]\n   475\t  (let [r (stream/cursor reader anchor)\n   476\t        outcome (:dao.stream/outcome r)]\n   477\t    (cond\n   478\t      (= :dao.stream/ok outcome) [:ok (:dao.stream/cursor r) r]\n   479\t\n   480\t      (and (= :dao.stream/transport-error outcome) (:dao.stream/retry? r))\n   481\t      [:retry anchor r]\n   482\t\n   483\t      :else [:terminal anchor r])))\n   484\t\n   485\t\n   486\t(defn- poll-read\n   487\t  [state]\n   488\t  (let [read-result (valid-operation-result :next (stream/next (:reader state) (:cursor state)))\n   489\t        outcome (:dao.stream/outcome read-result)]\n   490\t    (case outcome\n   491\t      :dao.stream/ok\n   492\t      ;; Advance before decoding. A malformed or unsolicited element is thereby\n   493\t      ;; consumed exactly once and cannot poison polling.\n   494\t      (handle-event (assoc state :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor read-result))\n   495\t                    (decode-value state (:dao.stream/value read-result)))\n   496\t\n   497\t      :dao.stream/blocked\n   498\t      (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/idle state :dao.stream.rpc/read read-result)\n   499\t\n   500\t      :dao.stream/gap\n   501\t      (let [state (-> state\n   502\t                      (assoc :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor read-result))\n   503\t                      (lose-outstanding :dao.stream/gap false))]\n   504\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/lost state\n   505\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/reason :dao.stream/gap\n   506\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/read read-result))\n   507\t\n   508\t      (terminal-lost state read-result))))\n   509\t\n   510\t\n   511\t(defn poll!\n   512\t  \"Read at most `budget` response-medium elements.\n   513\t\n   514\t   A `:dao.stream/blocked` result yields immediately; this function never loo