{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc | sed -n '511,590p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '430,453p;590,610p'; rg -n 'dao.stream.apply/call' src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc; rg -n 'apply2/(request|success-response|error-response|response\\\\?)' src/cljs/datomworld/demo* -g '*.cljs' | head\"","aggregated_output":"   511\t(defn poll!\n   512\t  \"Read at most `budget` response-medium elements.\n   513\t\n   514\t   A `:dao.stream/blocked` result yields immediately; this function never loops\n   515\t   waiting for a response.  Each successful read installs the exact successor\n   516\t   cursor returned by that handle before decoding the element.\"\n   517\t  ([state] (poll! state 1))\n   518\t  ([state budget]\n   519\t   (cond\n   520\t     (:terminal state) (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/terminal state)\n   521\t     (not (and (integer? budget) (pos? budget)))\n   522\t     (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/idle state)\n   523\t     :else\n   524\t     (loop [remaining budget\n   525\t            state state\n   526\t            last-result nil]\n   527\t       (cond\n   528\t         (zero? remaining) (or last-result (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/idle state))\n   529\t\n   530\t         ;; The response cursor is an unresolved anchor: settling it is not\n   531\t         ;; a read and does not spend the budget, exactly as\n   532\t         ;; dao.jing.content.step/poll's own anchor branch does not.\n   533\t         (contains? stream/standard-anchors (:cursor state))\n   534\t         (let [[status cursor r] (mint-cursor (:reader state) (:cursor state))]\n   535\t           (case status\n   536\t             :ok (recur remaining (assoc state :cursor cursor) last-result)\n   537\t             :retry (or last-result\n   538\t                        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/idle state :dao.stream.rpc/read r))\n   539\t             :terminal (terminal-lost state r)))\n   540\t\n   541\t         :else\n   542\t         (let [result (poll-read state)\n   543\t               next-state (:dao.stream.rpc/state result)\n   544\t               outcome (:dao.stream.rpc/outcome result)]\n   545\t           (if (or (:terminal next-state)\n   546\t                   (= outcome :dao.stream.rpc/idle))\n   547\t             result\n   548\t             (recur (dec remaining) next-state result))))))))\n   549\t\n   550\t\n   551\t(defn take-completed\n   552\t  \"Return `[completions next-state]`, clearing the unpublished completion\n   553\t   outbox exactly once.  The caller owns publication and must call this only\n   554\t   from its single state-owning driver step.\"\n   555\t  [state]\n   556\t  [(:completed state) (assoc state :completed [])])\n   557\t\n   558\t\n   559\t(defn take-diagnostics\n   560\t  \"Return `[diagnostics next-state]`, clearing the unpublished diagnostic outbox\n   561\t   exactly once.  Diagnostics are bounded by publication exactly as completions\n   562\t   are: a noisy shared response medium can produce one per read, so a caller\n   563\t   that never publishes would grow this vector for the session's lifetime.\"\n   564\t  [state]\n   565\t  [(:diagnostics state) (assoc state :diagnostics [])])\n   566\t\n   567\t\n   568\t(defn rebind\n   569\t  \"Replace a detached client's writer (and optionally reader) without creating\n   570\t   a cursor or replacing the random id allocator. Only `/detached` is\n   571\t   reconnectable; other terminal reasons remain terminal.\"\n   572\t  ([state writer]\n   573\t   (if (= :dao.stream.apply/detached (:terminal state))\n   574\t     (assoc state :writer writer :terminal nil)\n   575\t     state))\n   576\t  ([state writer reader]\n   577\t   (if (= :dao.stream.apply/detached (:terminal state))\n   578\t     (assoc state :writer writer :reader reader :terminal nil)\n   579\t     state)))\n   430\t- Endpoint-dependent mapping and filtering on both sides (a symmetric NAT;\n   431\t  the strict carrier-grade paths): punching is unreliable; relay through a\n   432\t  pair served by M.\n   433\t- UDP blocked: WebSocket only, to a reachable peer, and the browser rule\n   434\t  applies; without one, impossible (section 1).\n   435\t- Browser at one end: it dials, and two browsers relay through a pair. A\n   436\t  WebRTC data channel is a future channel type whose candidates ride the\n   437\t  same meeting stream.\n   438\t\n   439\t**Hole punching, as a convention.** M serves a `meet-requests` stream with\n   440\tsurface `#{:writer}` and a `meet-board` stream with surface `#{:reader}`,\n   441\tboth ordinary table entries. Over a UDP channel a peer appends\n   442\t`{:meet/here <name>}`; M's interpreter reads the request and the channel\n   443\tattachment identity it arrived on, which for UDP is the reflexive address,\n   444\tand appends `{:meet/seen <name> :meet/reflexive {:host h :port p}\n   445\t:dao.lease/lease L}` to the board. Both parties of a call read the board,\n   446\tthen each sends a `descriptor` request with a fresh id to the other's\n   447\treflexive address from the same socket M observed. A path exists when an\n   448\tanswer, even a `not-found` error, carrying one of this peer's outstanding\n   449\tids arrives from the expected address; the first inbound datagram alone\n   450\tconfirms nothing. That is the whole of STUN, as facts on a stream; no probe\n   451\tframe exists.\n   452\t\n   453\t**Bounded meeting work.** M's interpreter bounds its own work: the board's\n   590\ttuple space's current query value as the decision, and its query\n   591\tinterpreter is `verify`, a query over that value with the request as\n   592\tbindings, the shape `dao.space.query/q` has. `dao.shibi.md` states the\n   593\tadapter contract and its limits (one snapshot, bounded cost, no waiting);\n   594\trequest-specific decisions awaited over streams are a non-goal there.\n   595\t\n   596\tLease attribution uses per-author media; renewal gates are optional.\n   597\t\n   598\t## 8. Unification and contract boundary\n   599\t\n   600\t`dao.jing.content` succeeds `dao.jing.remote` whole. The implementation\n   601\tplan lists the successor vocabulary (section 1), the network-path fates\n   602\t(section 5) and the five `dao.stream.md` amendments (section 2), which add\n   603\tno network or capability concept.\n   604\t\n   605\t[impl-plan]: ./dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md\n194:    :dao.stream.apply/call (:operands node)\n578:   :dao.stream.apply/call 21,\n615:                    :dao.stream.apply/call 21,\n772:                  :dao.stream.apply/call\n773:                  (do (emit! e :yin/type :dao.stream.apply/call)\n869:       :dao.stream.apply/call (let [op (get-attr root-id :yin/op)\n994:   :dao.stream.apply/call [[:op :kw] [:operands :nodes]],\n1015:   [:dao.stream.apply/call :operands] []})\n1691:           :dao.stream.apply/call #{},\n1749:              :where [$ast _ :dao.stream.apply/call ?op _]],\n1758:                                   :dao.stream.apply/call}\n1967:   `:dao.stream.apply/call`.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-28 06:03:19 GMT  \nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-28 13:03:19 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\n## Ruling\n\nConverge **the application wire vocabulary** on `dao.stream.apply`. Keep `dao.stream.apply` as the open envelope contract and `dao.stream.rpc` as the client driver. This is a small breaking change while the rpc vocabulary is newly landed and gated; a compatibility shim would leave two accepted wire languages and prolong the drift this ruling is meant to end.\n\nThere are two corrections to the proposed direction. First, the present rpc client has random safe ID allocation and tracks outstanding requests, but it has **no numeric cap on the outstanding map**: `request!` allocates whenever there is no retained unsent request, and acceptance adds an entry to `:outstanding` without a size check ([rpc.cljc:212](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:212), [rpc.cljc:233](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:233), [rpc.cljc:254](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:254), [rpc.cljc:301](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:301)). Add an explicit, caller supplied positive outstanding limit, with a documented finite default. At the limit, return a local backpressure outcome without allocating an ID or appending a request. Second, “plain apply envelopes” describes the **values on the request and response streams**; the lower `dao.stream.remote` protocol still wraps stream operations in its own id, identity, and outcome envelope ([remote.cljc:48](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:48), [remote.cljc:138](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:138)). It must carry the apply value verbatim.\n\n## Target contract\n\n| Concern | Ruling |\n|---|---|\n| Request | `{:dao.stream.apply/id id :dao.stream.apply/op keyword :dao.stream.apply/args vector}`, with arbitrary additional keys. Use `apply/request?` on received values; preserve the entire map when forwarding or retaining it. This is the existing open contract ([apply.cljc:31](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:31), [apply.cljc:38](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:38)). |\n| Success | `{:dao.stream.apply/id id :dao.stream.apply/ok value}`; `nil` is a valid value ([apply.cljc:77](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:77), [apply.cljc:89](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:89)). |\n| Error | `{:dao.stream.apply/id id :dao.stream.apply/error {:dao.stream.apply/code qualified-keyword :dao.stream.apply/message string}}`, with additional envelope or error-map keys permitted ([apply.cljc:63](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:63), [apply.cljc:83](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:83), [apply.cljc:89](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:89)). |\n| Validation | rpc’s former `answer?` checks only safe ID and exclusive presence of `ok` or `error`; it does **not** validate the error body. The apply predicate does validate its qualified code and string message ([rpc.cljc:111](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:111), [apply.cljc:70](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:70)). Use `apply/response?` for decoding, then apply rpc’s safe-ID check at its client boundary. Malformed answers become rpc diagnostics and are consumed once, as its polling path already does ([rpc.cljc:358](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:358), [rpc.cljc:486](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:486)). |\n| Diagnostics and completions | Remain rpc-local events and state, outside the apply wire envelope. They record unsolicited or malformed responses, gaps, terminal loss, and completed work ([rpc.cljc:29](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:29), [rpc.cljc:192](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:192), [rpc.cljc:385](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:385), [rpc.cljc:486](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:486)). Their public output keys may stay rpc-namespaced; the embedded request and response values must be apply envelopes. |\n\nThe field mapping is lossless for application requests and answers: rpc `id/op/args`, `id/ok`, and `id/error/code/message` each have an apply counterpart ([rpc.cljc:71](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:71), [rpc.cljc:99](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:99), [apply.cljc:15](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:15)). The stricter ID domain and the event machinery are client policy, not missing application fields.\n\n**IDs.** Leave `apply/correlation-id?` as `some?`: the contract deliberately accepts opaque non-nil IDs and assigns cross-host allocation policy to rpc ([apply.cljc:24](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:24)). rpc continues to mint random integers in its cross-host safe range, reroll collisions against IDs still in use, and fail explicitly after its retry bound ([rpc.cljc:24](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:24), [rpc.cljc:35](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:35), [rpc.cljc:60](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:60), [rpc.cljc:144](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:144), [rpc.cljc:233](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:233)). Document and enforce `safe-id?` and the new outstanding cap **in rpc**, including on received answers. Do not reject another valid apply producer’s opaque ID at the apply layer.\n\n**Errors.** Keep transport or client failures as rpc completion reasons unless a server is actually sending an application error response. Define distinct, stable apply-qualified reason words for `not-found`, `detached`, `ended`, `no-surface`, `oversize`, and `transport-error`. Map remote `no-surface` to `:dao.stream.apply/no-surface` and remote `oversize` to `:dao.stream.apply/oversize`; do not collapse them into the generic transport reason. `no-surface`, `oversize`, `not-found`, and `ended` are terminal for that binding; `detached` alone permits rebind; a generic transport failure follows the existing terminal path. The current mapping distinguishes only `not-found` and `channel-gone`, while `rebind` already accepts only `detached` ([rpc.cljc:432](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:432), [rpc.cljc:445](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:445), [rpc.cljc:568](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:568)). The remote source already emits separate `no-surface` and `oversize` protocol errors ([remote.cljc:39](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:39), [remote.cljc:123](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:123), [remote.cljc:164](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:164)).\n\n**Identity.** An apply response requires its correlation ID, not a stream identity. Keep it that way: an application envelope can be used over a local pair with no remote identity ([apply.cljc:89](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:89)). A *remote protocol answer* already names `:dao.stream/identity`; its reflection must accept it only when **both** the ID and identity equal those of the outstanding request. Enforce that in `remote/absorb!` before removing the outstanding entry, marking a reflection gone, filing an answer, installing `more`, or emitting an append outcome. A mismatch is a diagnostic, leaving the legitimate request outstanding. The mirror echoes the request identity, but `absorb!` currently looks up only by ID ([remote.cljc:82](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:82), [remote.cljc:401](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:401)). This is correlation integrity within the remote protocol, not authentication of whoever can write to the channel.\n\n## Serving and end-to-end acceptance\n\nThe serving composition creates the call-in and call-out media, mints their cursors from their actual handles, registers each handle and declared surface in its remote mirror table, and retains that table, channel, cursor, and any lease for as long as the call can be resumed. The remote mirror serves stream operations; the FFI interpreter reads apply requests and appends apply responses. `apply/serve-once!` already takes caller-owned cursor state, retains a computed response across `full`, and never mints a cursor itself ([apply.cljc:217](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:217), [apply.cljc:301](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:301)). The mirror likewise holds only its returned channel cursor and forwards the served stream’s cursors ([remote.cljc:185](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:185)).\n\nSpecify the UCF `serve!` callback as **idempotent per live handle within one export binding**: repeated calls return the same identity and channel and refer to the same table entry; retirement or lease reclaim ends that binding explicitly. `lift-frame` can call `serve!` while minting cells and again while lifting entries, so unstable markers would break a single frame ([ucf/remote.cljc:217](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:217), [ucf/remote.cljc:435](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:435)). Implement a production composition that owns this registry and its lease lifecycle; the UCF facade only accepts the callback and makes remote descriptors from it ([ucf/remote.cljc:37](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:37), [ucf/remote.cljc:144](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:144)). A refusal returns `nil` before publishing a lift, as the facade specifies ([ucf/remote.cljc:41](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:41)).\n\n“VM FFI call answered remotely, end-to-end” is accepted only when a real VM call emits its original apply request; the remote serving composition exposes its call-out request stream to an rpc-driven responder; that responder reads the **unchanged** request, appends a valid apply success or error to the response stream; and the VM resumes or raises the corresponding error through `yin.vm.ffi/call-result`, with the same ID and any extra request keys preserved where the request is retained or transported. Verify pending append retries, response `full`, gap, detach and rebind, terminal not-found, and UCF lift/lower of a retained call. The VM already constructs apply requests and checks apply response correlation ([semantic.cljc:467](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:467), [ffi.cljc:88](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc:88)); UCF already carries a retained request envelope verbatim ([ucf/remote.cljc:328](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:328)).\n\n## Migration order\n\n1. **Contract and driver, one breaking commit.** Replace rpc request and answer constructors, predicates, and accessors with apply equivalents at the wire boundary. Preserve rpc’s client state machine, event names, completion API, ID allocator, and cursor minting. Add the outstanding cap and the distinct error reasons. Migrate rpc tests and the REPL adapter/server tests together. A request emitted by `rpc/request!` and an answer accepted by `rpc/poll!` must pass the apply predicates; rpc must reject an invalid error body and diagnose an unsafe reply ID. Current wire consumers include `yin.repl.adapter` and `yin.repl.serve` ([adapter.cljc:50](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/adapter.cljc:50), [adapter.cljc:169](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/adapter.cljc:169), [serve.cljc:424](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc:424), [serve.cljc:503](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc:503)). `yin.repl.connect` creates and rebinds the driver, and `yin.repl.driver` reads its completion IDs; update their assertions where affected ([connect.cljc:422](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc:422), [connect.cljc:516](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc:516), [driver.cljc:383](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/driver.cljc:383)). The polling observation layer documents rpc and apply as separate state transitions, not separate wire contracts ([observe.cljc:28](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/observe.cljc:28)).\n2. **Remote correlation and channel loss.** Add the identity check in `remote/absorb!`. Make a pair reader’s in-stream `not-found` or `channel-gone` end that pair binding and propagate loss to the outer link, as it already does for gap and end ([remote_pair.cljc:58](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote_pair.cljc:58), [remote_pair.cljc:95](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote_pair.cljc:95), [remote.cljc:443](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:443)). Acceptance: a wrong-identity answer cannot complete a request, and a dead pair cannot leave waiters polling forever.\n3. **Production FFI serving composition.** Supply the stable per-handle `serve!` registry, cursor and reflection ownership, lease retirement, and a remote responder using apply values. Demonstrate the end-to-end VM case above, including UCF retained-envelope round trip.\n\nThe correlation check and pair termination are independent remote-core fixes and may land before slice 1. The `no-surface`/`oversize` mapping and outstanding cap belong to convergence’s driver acceptance. Stable `serve!`, leased ownership, and the end-to-end case are required before claiming **remote FFI** complete; they need not block the wire-vocabulary commit.\n\nDo **not** rename yin’s `dao.stream.apply/call` syntax or AST: that name is parsed and used throughout the VM ([yin.vm.cljc:194](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:194), [semantic.cljc:552](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:552)). Do **not** rebuild VM requests from op and args, tighten apply’s open predicates to rpc’s allocation domain, or add rpc event fields to the application envelope. The VM already emits apply requests, the apply protocol admits additional keys, and UCF explicitly retains the whole envelope because rebuilding it loses those keys ([semantic.cljc:467](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:467), [apply.cljc:38](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:38), [ucf/remote.cljc:328](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:328)).\n\nVerdict: ADOPTED WITH CHANGES"}}
